16.text:
In the competitive equilibrium $P_{S}(Q)=P_{D}(Q)$

\begin{equation*}
\ln \!\left(Q^{3}-2\right)=18\,
\exp\!\left[-\arctan Q\right]\equiv P(Q) 
\end{equation*}

is monotone increasing on the left and monotone decreasing on the right.
The (real) root is

* $Q^{*}\simeq 4.6\!-\!4.7$ ,
* $t^{*}=\arctan Q^{*}\simeq1.36$,

and

\begin{equation*}
P^{*}= \ln \!\left({Q^{*}}^{3}-2\right)
      =18e^{-t^{*}}\simeq 4.6\!-\!4.7\approx 4.62 ,
\end{equation*}
$Q^{*}\approx 4.69$ in the usual numerical solution, $P^{*}\approx4.61$,
which is the price at which the two curves cross.

Put

\begin{equation*}
D(Q)=18 e^{-\arctan Q},\qquad S(Q)=\ln\!\left(Q^{3}-2\right)
\end{equation*}

and denote by $Q_{0}$ the (economic) origin of the supply curve,
$S(Q_{0})=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\ln\!\left(Q_{0}^{3}-2\right)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
Q_{0}=\root3\of{3}\approx1.44 ,
\end{equation*}
so that $S(Q)\ge 0$ for $Q\ge Q_{0}$.
With the usual definitions

\begin{align*}
CS &=\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}\!\big[\,D(Q)-P^{*}\,\big]\,dQ
    =I_{D}(Q^{*})-P^{*}Q^{*},\\
PS &=\int_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}\!\big[\,P^{*}-S(Q)\,\big]\,dQ
    =P^{*}\!\left(Q^{*}-Q_{0}\right)-J(Q^{*}),
\end{align*}

where

\begin{equation*}
I_{D}(Q^{*})=\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}18e^{-\arctan Q}dQ
      =\int_{0}^{t^{*}}18e^{-t}\sec^{2}t\,dt ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
J(Q^{*})=\int_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}\ln\!\left(Q^{3}-2\right)dQ .
\end{equation*}

Numerically

\begin{equation*}
I_{D}(Q^{*})\approx 3.24\times10^{1},\qquad 
J(Q^{*})\approx 9.86 ,
\end{equation*}

and with $Q^{*}\approx4.69$, $P^{*}\approx4.61\!-\!4.62$,

\begin{equation*}
CS =I_{D}-P^{*}Q^{*}\approx 32.4-21.6\approx 10.8,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
PS =P^{*}(Q^{*}-Q_{0})-J\approx 14.98-9.86\approx5.1,
\end{equation*}

i.e. in the economically meaningful $S\ge0$ region

\begin{equation*}
W_{e}=CS+PS
     =I_{D}-P^{*}Q_{0}-J
     \approx 1.6\times10^{1}
\end{equation*}

on the $P\!-\!Q$ scale, about $CS\approx 10.8$ and $PS\approx5.1$, $W_{e}\approx
15\!-\!16$, $Q^{*}\approx4.7$, $P^{*}\approx4.6$, which is the area between
the demand curve and the (non-negative) supply curve up to the
competitive point.

If the area under the demand curve is taken from $0$ to $Q^{*}$ and the
area under the supply curve is taken from its economic origin $Q_{0}$ to
$Q^{*}$ the familiar “area between the curves’’ is

\begin{equation*}
W_{b}= \int_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}\!\!\big[\,D(Q)-S(Q)\,\big]dQ
     =I_{D}(Q^{*})-P^{*}Q_{0}-J(Q^{*})+P^{*}Q_{0}
     \approx I_{D}(Q^{*})-J(Q^{*})\approx 22.6,
\end{equation*}

the $32.4-9.9\approx22.6$ net area under $D$ above $S$ up to $Q^{*}$.
The $W_{e}\approx15.9$ quoted above is $W_{b}$ net of the $P^{*}Q_{0}$
rectangle which is normally removed when $CS$ and $PS$ are defined in the
usual way.  This is the conventional total surplus at the $Q^{*}\approx
4.69,\;P^{*}\approx4.62$ competitive equilibrium.

